WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.890
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
Last time, we covered

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:04.230
how you can use Google Cloud
platform metrics as Service

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:07.200
Level Indicators to measure the
reliability of your services

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.330
and set reliability
targets using SLOs.

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:13.080
But what do you do if you need
custom telemetry for your SLIs?

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.330
Today, I'm going to show
you how to use Prometheus

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.580
and OpenCensus to
write custom metrics

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:20.040
and define SLIs and
SLOs using them.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.980
Welcome to Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud.

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:26.830
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:29.860
Imagine a basic web application
architecture like this.

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:32.380
While we can use the metrics
emitted by the load balancer

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:35.440
to track things like request
counts and response latency,

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.270
what if our application
running on GKE

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:39.730
needs to keep track of
specific transaction details

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:41.553
and write a custom
counter for them?

00:00:41.553 --> 00:00:43.720
What if we want to use this
kind of custom telemetry

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:45.670
as our SLI for the service?

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:47.740
This is where custom
metrics come in.

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:49.390
Today, I'm going to
show you how to use

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:52.330
two open-source approaches
to custom metric telemetry.

00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:54.160
The first one is Prometheus.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:55.870
Prometheus is an
open-source systems

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:57.890
monitoring alerting tool
kit, originally built

00:00:57.890 --> 00:00:58.820
with SoundCloud.

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:01.000
It has a very active
developer and user community

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.208
and joined the Cloud Native
Computing Foundation back

00:01:03.208 --> 00:01:04.342
in 2016.

00:01:04.342 --> 00:01:05.800
This is the
architecture Prometheus

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:07.318
uses to collect metrics.

00:01:07.318 --> 00:01:08.860
The general idea is
that your service

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:11.710
needs to expose a dedicated
metrics endpoint, which

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:14.230
Prometheus will then scrape
to collect metric data.

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:16.420
That data is stored in
the time series database

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:19.682
and can be queried through
a web UI or an API.

00:01:19.682 --> 00:01:21.890
We'll link to the full code
sample in the show notes,

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:23.680
but let's have a look
at how to instrument

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:27.070
an application written in NodeJS
to emit counter and histogram

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:28.180
metrics.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:30.280
Start by defining the
metrics themselves.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:33.100
For each, specify the
metric type, its name,

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:34.660
and a help message
that will describe

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:36.430
what the metric is capturing.

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:38.590
To track latency,
use a histogram,

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:40.270
and call its observe method.

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:43.220
To increment counters,
call their inc method.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:46.720
Finally, create a dedicated
endpoint in your application

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:49.640
that Prometheus will use
to scrape for metrics.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:51.430
The instrumentation is done.

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:54.340
Now, if you go to the
/metrics endpoint in your app,

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:56.230
you'll see a page
that looks like this.

00:01:56.230 --> 00:01:58.630
The metrics and their
values are written out here.

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:00.190
Now you need to
configure Prometheus

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:02.500
itself to scrape this endpoint.

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:04.390
You do that by adding
some annotations

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:06.700
in the deployments spec
in your application,

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:09.070
enable scraping,
and define the port

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:11.560
and endpoint where the
metrics are available.

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:15.388
Next, you need to ingest these
metrics into Cloud Monitoring.

00:02:15.388 --> 00:02:17.680
We'll link the detailed
instructions in the show notes,

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:20.200
but the basic idea is that you
pass your Prometheus server

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:22.750
with a collector that will
send these metrics to Cloud

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:23.830
monitoring.

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:25.670
Once your metrics
are available there,

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:28.537
you're ready to use
them for SLIs and SLOs.

00:02:28.537 --> 00:02:31.120
Here's an example of how you can
use a latency metric, written

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:34.150
by Prometheus, to create
a distribution-cut SLI

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:36.430
and set an SLO target for it.

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:38.890
A distribution cut allows
you to capture the percentage

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:40.570
of values that fall
within a specified

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:43.055
threshold, as defined by range.

00:02:43.055 --> 00:02:46.570
This SLO expects
that 98% of responses

00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:51.850
complete within 500 milliseconds
over a rolling 28-day period.

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:54.040
Prometheus is a popular
open-source monitoring

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:56.680
framework, and it's well suited
for monitoring applications

00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:57.797
running in GKE.

00:02:57.797 --> 00:02:59.380
But what if you don't
want to maintain

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:00.950
that additional infrastructure?

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:03.010
In that case, you
can use OpenCensus

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:06.400
to write metrics directly using
the Cloud Monitoring exporter.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.170
OpenCensus is a
single distribution

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:10.600
of libraries to collect
metrics and distributed traces

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:11.973
from your services.

00:03:11.973 --> 00:03:14.140
Let's have a look at how
to instrument your service,

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:15.880
configure the Cloud
Monitoring exporter,

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:20.330
and use OpenCensus metrics
to define SLIs and SLOs.

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:23.297
Again, we'll link to the full
code samples in the show notes,

00:03:23.297 --> 00:03:25.130
but let's take a quick
look at instrumenting

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:28.370
an application written in NodeJS
to emit counter and histogram

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:30.780
metrics.

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:33.250
Start by configuring the
Cloud Monitoring exporter.

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:35.250
This will send the metrics
you captured directly

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:37.292
to Cloud Monitoring, where
you will then use them

00:03:37.292 --> 00:03:39.990
to create SLIs and define SLOs.

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:42.600
Next, define a measure
for each metric.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:44.250
Measures describe
how the data points

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:45.960
themselves will be recorded.

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:47.970
Create a view for each measure.

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:49.530
Views are basically
the [INAUDIBLE]

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:51.150
you'll be adding data points to.

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:52.950
They also include
information about how

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:54.870
that data will be aggregated.

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:56.730
For our latency
histogram, you'll

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:58.770
need to specify your
bucket boundaries.

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:00.270
It's important to
at least make sure

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:01.687
that you have
buckets that capture

00:04:01.687 --> 00:04:05.340
requests that are going to fall
within and outside of your SLO.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.440
However, you'll likely
want more than just

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:11.360
those to allow you to, for
example, measure PINI latency.

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:14.050
Finally, record values
using the record method.

00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:15.680
Unlike Prometheus,
this is the same

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:18.260
for counters and
histograms in OpenCensus.

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:20.060
You won't need to
configure any scraping.

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:21.920
The exporter will write
the values directly

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:23.270
to Cloud Monitoring.

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:26.310
You can then use them to
define SLOs like this.

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:28.090
Here's an example of
a distribution cut

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:31.880
SLI using the response latency
metric, written by OpenCensus,

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:35.330
with an SLO of 98% of
responses being completed

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:38.530
in under 1,000 milliseconds.

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:39.760
Thanks for joining me today.

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:42.135
In this episode, we looked at
how to instrument your code

00:04:42.135 --> 00:04:44.740
to write custom metrics with
OpenCensus and Prometheus,

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:47.080
use them to define
Service Level Indicators,

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:50.020
and set Service Level
Objectives against those.

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:51.970
You're now ready to
start defining SLIs

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:55.270
by using these open-source
libraries in your own services.

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:58.150
In our next episode, we'll
look at alerting on our SLOs

00:04:58.150 --> 00:04:59.823
with error budget burn rates.

00:04:59.823 --> 00:05:01.990
Don't forget to like and
subscribe to never miss out

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:04.930
on more Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud.

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:05.560
See you soon.

